F. Synthesis
  The ordinary full deck is a deletion projection; through n=6 it still
  has small collision buckets.  Kelly-style subtournament counts match
  the full-deck collision strength in this range but do not repair it.
  The useful repairs are marked derivative channels.  The cleanest one
  checked here is paired deleted score: attach to each card the outdegree
  of the missing vertex.  Unpaired deleted-score multisets do not suffice
  at n=6, and global H or score sequences do not repair the n=6 collision
  buckets.  Paired score+C3-loss and S658's stronger H/C3/score loss deck
  also resolve every checked collision.  This is the tournament analogue
  of retaining owner/carry
  in LRC, point-deletion degree/gain in unit distance, set pressure in
  union-closed families, and pinned/concurrency labels in finite-field
  Kakeya/Falconer carriers.
  The n=7 extension should use canonical augmentation or nauty-style
  automorphism pruning rather than raw permutation canonicalization.
